Implement the solution. Modifier: (score - 10) / 2 rounded down (floor). In Go, integer division truncates toward zero, so for negative values we need to floor it: e.g., score 3 → (3-10)/2 = -7/2 = -3 (truncated), but the expected value is -4. So use floor: (score - 10 + ((score-10)%2 != 0 ? ... )). More simply: `(score - 10) / 2` doesn't work for negative values. Use `(score - 10) / 2 - (score%2 != 0 && score < 10 ? 1 : 0)`? Actually, in D&D, modifier = floor((score-10)/2). For score 3: floor(-7/2) = -4. In Go: -7/2 = -3 (truncation). So we need to adjust: if (score-10) is odd and negative, subtract 1.

Clean way to write it:
```go
func Modifier(score int) int {
    mod := (score - 10) / 2
    if (score-10) < 0 && (score-10)%2 != 0 {
        mod--
    }
    return mod
}
```

